title: Subtracting numbers
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
`-` works on `unsigned` numbers just as `+` does. With 4 bits there are no negative numbers, so 3 − 5 **wraps round**, like a car's odometer going backwards past zero: 3 − 5 gives 14 (16 − 2).

As with the adder, read the inputs as numbers with `unsigned(...)` and turn the answer back into bits with `std_logic_vector(...)`.
---- task
Make `d` the 4-bit difference `a` − `b`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity sub4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    b : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    d : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end sub4;

architecture rtl of sub4 is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity sub4 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    b : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    d : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end sub4;

architecture rtl of sub4 is
begin
    d <= std_logic_vector(unsigned(a) - unsigned(b));
end rtl;
---- hint1
Like the adder, with `-` instead of `+`, and no `resize` this time.
---- hint2
`d <= std_logic_vector(unsigned(a) - unsigned(b));`
---- solution
`d <= std_logic_vector(unsigned(a) - unsigned(b));` Try 3 − 5: the answer is 1110, which is 14.
---- done
Wrapping round is how computers really store negative numbers: in 4 bits, 1110 can mean −2. That is called **two's complement**.
